{"builder":"Goal: when a config file is malformed, the error names the file path and the line and column of the mistake. Malformed config must still stop the app; do NOT make `loadConfig` catch the error and return `{}` or defaults (the issue's suggested fix is rejected).\n\nCurrent code:\n- `src/config.js`: `loadConfig(path)` returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`.\n- `src/app.js`: calls `loadConfig(process.argv[2] ?? 'config.json')`, catches errors, writes `bad config: ${error.message}` to stderr and exits 1; otherwise prints `listening on <port>`.\n- `test/config.test.js`: tests run with `npm test` (`node --test`). The existing test `bad JSON throws` expects a `SyntaxError`; either keep throwing a SyntaxError (e.g. a new SyntaxError with the original as `cause`) or update that test.\n\nSteps:\n1. In `src/config.js`, wrap the `JSON.parse` call in try/catch. On a parse error, throw a new error (do not return) whose `message` contains the path exactly as passed in (do not resolve it to an absolute path) plus the line and column of the mistake, e.g. `<path>:3:3: Expected double-quoted property name ...` or `<path>: ... (line 3 column 3)`.\n2. Compute line and column yourself rather than relying only on Node's message: take the `position N` from the SyntaxError message if present and convert it to a 1-based line/column by counting newlines in the file text before that offset. If Node's message already contains `(line L column C)` you may use those numbers. If neither is available, still include the path. Keep the original error as `cause`.\n3. Leave file-read errors (e.g. ENOENT) as they are; they must still end as an exit-1 `bad config:` failure in `src/app.js`. Leave `src/app.js`'s output format `bad config: <message>` as is unless a change is needed.\n4. Valid configs must load exactly as before: `loadConfig` on `{\"port\": 9000}` returns `{ port: 9000 }`, and `node src/app.js <file>` prints `listening on 9000` and exits 0.\n5. Add tests in `test/config.test.js`: (a) `loadConfig` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws and the error `message` includes the file path; (b) for a file with the four lines `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}`, the message includes the path and line 3 / column 3; optionally (c) spawn `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json` and assert exit 1, empty stdout, and stderr containing `bad config:`, the path, 3 and 3. Test (a) must fail on the current code.\n6. Check by hand under Node 22: create a tmp dir `d`, write c.json with the four lines above, run `node src/app.js $d/c.json`; expect exit 1, no stdout, stderr containing `bad config:`, `$d/c.json`, line 3 and column 3. Run it with `{\"port\": 9000}` too and expect `listening on 9000`, exit 0. Run `npm test` and make sure it passes.\n7. Optionally update the README line to mention that the message names the file and location.","verifier":"Work at the PR head. Use Node 22 (`node --version`). In each check, `d` is a fresh `mktemp -d` directory.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \\\"port\\\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \\\"x\\\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json >$d/out 2>$d/err; echo \"exit $?\"; cat $d/out`\nPASS: exit code is 1 and `$d/out` is empty. FAIL: any other exit code, or anything on stdout.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.\"\nRun: the same setup as Check 1, then `cat $d/err`.\nPASS: stderr contains `bad config:`, contains the string `$d/c.json` exactly as passed, and identifies line 3 and column 3 of the mistake in any readable form (e.g. `line 3 column 3`, `3:3`, `line 3, col 3`). FAIL: any of these four missing, or the line/column given is not 3 and 3.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > $d/b.json; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"`\nPASS: prints `OK`. FAIL: prints `RETURNED ...` or `NO PATH: ...`.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\\\"port\\\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\"\nRun: `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; const v = loadConfig('$d/ok.json'); assert.deepEqual(v, {port: 9000}); console.log('EQUAL', JSON.stringify(v))\"`\nPASS: stdout of the app contains `listening on 9000`, exit is 0, and the second command prints `EQUAL {\"port\":9000}`. FAIL: any of these not true.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\"\nRun: `npm test` at the PR head. Then find the merge base (`git merge-base HEAD <default branch>`), and in a scratch copy (e.g. `git worktree add` at the PR head in a tmp dir) run `git checkout <base> -- src` and `npm test`; afterwards remove the scratch worktree.\nPASS: `npm test` passes at the PR head, and with the base commit's `src/` at least one test fails, and reading it shows it checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path. FAIL: `npm test` fails at head, or no test fails against base `src/`, or no failing test checks the file path in the malformed-JSON error.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail):\n- Read `src/config.js` to see whether line/column are computed from the file text or only taken from Node's message; note it if they depend only on Node's message, since other Node versions may not print them.\n- Note if `loadConfig` swallows errors for any input, or if a missing config file no longer ends in exit 1 with `bad config:` (`node src/app.js $d/missing.json`).\n- Note whether the path in the message is resolved to an absolute path rather than shown as given."}